## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical SRE skills: incident triage, region-aware rollouts, backpressure, circuit breaking, and rollback discipline beyond basic incident response.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-region latency analysis
- Cache stampede mitigation
- Per-region deployment strategies (canary, rollouts)
- Safe rollback criteria and instrumentation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple circuit breaker (pseudo)
class CircuitBreaker {
  constructor(threshold) { this.open = false; this.failures = 0; this.threshold = threshold; }
  onFailure() { this.failures++; if (this.failures > this.threshold) this.open = true; }
  allow() { return !this.open; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to a service mesh with traffic shifting?
- How would you verify rollback correctness across all regions post-incident?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design per-tenant budgets, dynamic throttling, and robust rollback strategies in a multi-tenant, latency-sensitive service.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant SLOs and budgets
- Burn rate calculation and budget ledger
- Edge throttling (token bucket) and graceful degradation
- Canary rollouts and automated rollback
- Observability with per-tenant metrics and PromQL

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: update per-tenant budget on each request
function onRequest(req){
  const tenant = req.headers['tenant-id'];
  const now = Date.now();
  const budget = ledger.getBudget(tenant);
  if (budget.remaining <= 0) throttle(req, tenant);
  else { forward(req); ledger.consume(tenant, estimateCost(req)); }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test this system under simulated traffic bursts?
- What thresholds would you choose for alerting and auto-rollback cadence?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes cross-region consistency, edge-scale reliability, and clear runbooks for failures.

## Key Concepts
- Distributed consensus with multi-DC Raft and quorum tuning
- Partition tolerance and per-tenant sharding
- Idempotent, sequenced operations
- Anti-entropy reconciliation and safe replay
- Observability, alerting, and rollback runbooks

## Code Example
```javascript
// simple idempotent write with sequence tokens
function applyWrite(store, key, value, token) {
  const cur = store[key];
  if (!cur || token > cur.token) {
    store[key] = { value, token };
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the reconciliation path under partitions?
- What metrics signal reconciliation backlog and potential data loss?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to craft an actionable, scalable incident response for a complex streaming pipeline with multi-tenant QoS, data correctness guarantees, and real-time rollback requirements.

## Key Concepts
- Observability: per-tenant backlog metrics, distributed traces, structured logs
- Backpressure handling: tenant-aware throttling, dynamic rebalancing
- Data correctness: idempotency, exactly-once semantics, DLQ handling
- Rollback safety: canary, traffic shaping, validated resumption

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-idempotent write with dedupe key
async function process(msg){
  const id = msg.id;
  if(await dedupe.has(id)) return;
  await warehouse.write(msg.payload);
  await dedupe.set(id, true, TTL);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the roll-forward vs rollback paths?
- Which metrics define success for resuming traffic, and how are tenants verified?
